      "source": "blog",
      "name": "CNCF Blog",
      "title": "OpenKruise Agents v0.6.0: Leaping ahead in agent Sandbox security and automation",
      "url": "https://www.cncf.io/blog/2026/11/03/openkruise-agents-v0-6-0-leaping-ahead-in-agent-sandbox-security-and-automation",
      "publishedAt": "2026-11-03T12:00:00+00:00",
      "author": "",
      "description": "",
      "content": "Posted on November 3, 2026 by Zhen Zhang, OpenKruise Maintainer CNCF projects highlighted in this post About OpenKruise Agents OpenKruise Agents is a sub-project of OpenKruise, the CNCF open-source application automation and workload orchestration project. It provides full lifecycle management for Agent Sandboxes running at scale on Kubernetes, together with a complete runtime environment covering networking, storage, and identity. OpenKruise Agents exposes both Kubernetes and E2B interfaces , so infrastructure teams, platform teams, and AI algorithm engineers can all operate sandboxes conveniently. The v0.3.0 release answered one question: once a Sandbox is handed to an Agent, how do you upgrade it, resize it, and account for it. Its roadmap closed with two promises — network governance through TrafficPolicy / SecurityProfile , and a security identity integration that keeps platform credentials out of Agent code. OpenKruise Agents v0.6.0 delivers both, and adds the operational machinery around them: 283 merged pull requests and 27 new contributors since v0.3.0. This post covers four capabilities in depth: L4 and L7 network access control plus token management — destination reachability, per-request inspection, MCP tool-level ACLs, and credential injection from Kubernetes Secrets; SandboxUpdateOps with CheckpointRestore and paused-instance upgrades — batch-upgrade a hibernated fleet without waking it by hand, and without losing the filesystem; PoolAutoscaler — declarative warm pool capacity with watermarks and cron schedules; Automatic pause and resume — probe-driven idle detection, scheduled wake, and wake-on-traffic, so idle Sandboxes stop consuming CPU. Together these deliver three kinds of customer value: security hardening , operational capability , and cost optimization . The post is organized in three parts: the architecture changes that made them possible, a deep dive into the four capabilities, and the value summary and roadmap. Part One: What Changed in the Architecture If v0.3.0 was a workload layer, v0.6.0 is a workload layer with its own data plane . Traffic inspection, policy distribution, and identity all became first-class components rather than external add-ons. The overall architecture: OpenKruise Agents v0.6.0 overall architecture From four components to six. v0.6.0 adds traffic-control-plane and traffic-proxy : Component Role in v0.6.0 sandbox-manager Stateless backend serving the E2B-compatible northbound API; owns sandbox lifecycle, quota, and route & traffic-token orchestration sandbox-controller Reconciles Sandbox, SandboxSet, SandboxClaim and warm pools; hosts admission webhooks; drives the automatic pause/resume controller sandbox-gateway Data plane built on Envoy Go filters. The ingress gateway proxies user traffic (host/path routing, access tokens, wake-on-traffic); the egress gateway enforces policy-driven L7 egress traffic-control-plane (new) istiod distributes xDS configuration to gateways and in-pod proxies; the Egress Policy Enforcer (EPE) applies L7 policy, MCP ACL, and token injection on egress traffic agent-runtime Sidecar providing E2B envd-compatible command, file, and port (49983) operations traffic-proxy (new) Sidecar acting as the in-pod L4 enforcement point for egress traffic policies Two consequences are worth internalizing. First, egress is no longer the Pod&#8217;s own business — it is captured in-pod, routed through an egress gateway, and decided by a policy engine that sees the full request, not just the socket. Second, the ingress gateway can wake a sandbox , which is what makes wake-on-traffic possible without an external poller. what makes wake-on-traffic possible without an external poller. The CRD count doubled from 6 to 12 , adding PoolAutoscaler , TrafficPolicy , GlobalTrafficPolicy , SecurityProfile , and GlobalSecurityProfile — a multi-tier control system spanning cluster, tenant, and sandbox scopes. Part Two: The Four Core Capabilities 2.1 Network Access Control and Token Management This is the largest change in the release. It answers three questions: what a Sandbox may access , who may access a Sandbox , and — the question most platforms get wrong — who actually holds the upstream API key . The data plane uses a two-stage traffic takeover, node-side L4 plus central L7 , balancing performance and function: Sandbox application container -> traffic-proxy (in-pod L4 capture; Deny hits are blocked in place) -> egress gateway (central gateway chosen by egress routing) -> Envoy external processing (ext_proc) -> Egress Policy Enforcer decision (L7 / MCP ACL / credential injection) -> external service, or a blocked response L4 filtering completes node-side with very low latency; L7 deep inspection is centralized in the gateway for unified operations, observability, and security audit. The split is deliberate: L4 denials are dropped before traffic reaches the gateway, and L7 can never override an L4 denial. L4: is this destination reachable at all TrafficPolicy selects caller Pods and holds an ordered egress rule list; peers support fqdn , cidr , service , and workload . Three semantics matter more than the syntax: Once a policy selects a Pod, egress traffic matching no allow rule is denied — a policy is a whitelist once active; Rules with a missing peer field are skipped by the compiler and do not form a default deny — a typo in to silently weakens the policy; Always allow the cluster DNS Service , or every FQDN rule will look broken. apiVersion: agents.kruise.io/v1alpha1 kind: TrafficPolicy metadata: name: sandbox-egress spec: selector: matchLabels: app: traffic-sample egress: rules: - action: allow to: - service: {name: kube-dns, namespace: kube-system} - action: allow to: - fqdn: api.example.com - action: reject ports: [{protocol: TCP, port: 443}] to: - cidr: 10.0.0.0/8 L7: what is this specific request allowed to do SecurityProfile matches HTTP requests and applies actions. block and bypass are terminating; mcpToolPolicy , headerManipulation, tokenTransformation, and audit are not. The highest-value use for Agent workloads is SSRF protection — prompt-injected code almost always tries internal services and cloud metadata endpoints: apiVersion: agents.kruise.io/v1alpha1 kind: GlobalSecurityProfile metadata: name: cluster-baseline spec: selector: matchLabels: {app: traffic-sample} priority: 50 # cluster baseline deliberately ordered before tenant profiles rules: - name: deny-metadata-endpoint match: - domains: - metadata.internal - \"*.internal.company.com\" actions: block: statusCode: 403 body: '{\"error\":\"access to internal services is forbidden\"}' MCP tool-level ACL When an Agent talks to an MCP Server, domain-level control is too coarse; the real risk is a specific tool . mcpToolPolicy governs only JSON-RPC tools/call and matches params.name exactly. Use defaultAction : deny for a whitelist. Credential injection: your API key never enters the Sandbox Credentials live in Kubernetes Secrets; the egress proxy resolves them per request and rewrites the outbound request as it leaves the cluster. Workloads and Agent code only ever see their own placeholder header. apiVersion: agents.kruise.io/v1alpha1 kind: SecurityProfile metadata: name: example-api-credentials namespace: agent-demo spec: selector: matchLabels: {app: agent} rules: - name: inject-example-api-key match: - domains: [api.example.com] schemes: [https] paths: [{type: Prefix, value: /v1/}] actions: tokenTransformation: type: ApiKey failStrategy: Block # fail closed when the credential is missing credentialRef: secret: name: example-api-credentials apiKey: targetHeaders: names: [authorization] value: template: \"Bearer {{ .Token }}\" Because resolution happens proxy-side, credentials never appear in Pod specs, environment variables, or Sandbox metadata, and rotating a Secret takes effect without restarting the Sandbox . Core performance metrics The traffic-control data plane is built around sandbox-gateway and has been validated at production sc..."
